{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And he fought against Israel.</b> The reason the verse says <b>against Israel</b> and does not say ״with Israel,״ as it says in the portion of Beshallah, ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק וַיִּלָּחֶם עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 17:8), is that it intends to say that they themselves were the cause of this war on account of their sin. Also, what it says immediately afterward, <b>and he took from him captives</b>, means that from him — from themselves — was the cause for him to take captives, and the sin is that they were the cause for the loss of the righteous one on account of the waters of Meribah, as it is written (Psalms 106:32): ״וַיַּקְרִיפוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה וַיֵּרַע לְמֹשֶׁה בַּעֲבוּרָם״ — and not to Moses alone, but also to Aaron, as is understood from the matter, except that it mentions Moses because he is the principal." "<b>And he took from him captives, etc.</b> There is a point to raise: why were Israel not alarmed when they saw that a single people took captives from Israel, and they did not say, ״If one people has broken through against them, what will happen when they enter the land?״ — as we find that Joshua was shaken when the men of Ai struck down one man of Israel, as stated there (Joshua 7:4). And even though afterward they intensified in prayer and HaShem delivered them into their hand, ultimately we do not find that they were shaken beforehand." "And it appears, according to the words of the Sages (Numbers Rabbah 19:20) who said that this people was Amalek — and even though Scripture called him a Canaanite, it was because he came in Canaanite garb — therefore when Israel prayed that He deliver him into their hand, they did not say ״deliver this Canaanite,״ [and] consequently, since he was not from the seven nations that HaShem had given to them, they were not shaken when they saw him victorious in battle, for after all they have nothing from him. Further, it appears that they were shaken only once they entered the land, when the time arrived to repay the iniquity of the Amorite, unlike before they entered the land, when it is possible that his iniquity was not yet full to deliver them into Israel's hand. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded many interpretations on this."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:40.070742978Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}